<resources>
    <!-- Pre-Compose window background, matching the Arctic (light) surface so launch
         shows the brand colour instead of a white flash. Compose drives all in-app colour. -->
    <color name="gridlink_window_background">#F7F8FA</color>
    <!-- Splash field. GridlinkDayColors.background, and it has to be: the splash hands straight
         over to GridlinkIntroOverlay, which paints Day's cyan-to-blue gradient corner to corner.

         🔴 This was #E9EFF7, the launcher tile's pale colour, and that was right only while the
         splash drew the mark on it — an exact tile match made the icon look like it was growing
         into the app. Now that the splash is bare and the intro paints the app's real backdrop,
         a pale field in front of that gradient is a white flash followed by saturated blue.
         Verified on the emulator: the jump is the most visible thing in the whole launch.

         ⚠️ #2F6FE0 is the gradient's flat FALLBACK, not its start (#4DD5F0). The gradient runs
         top-left to bottom-right and is this blue over most of the screen; matching the cyan
         corner instead would fix one corner and mismatch the other nine tenths.

         ⚠️ Imperfect by construction, and knowingly so. This resource follows the system's
         light/dark setting while the intro's palette follows the CLOCK (GridlinkTokens.
         gridlinkModeForHour), and no resource qualifier can read the hour. It is exact for the
         two cases that actually happen — light theme during Day hours, dark theme at night —
         and briefly wrong for a dark-themed phone at noon. -->
    <color name="gridlink_splash_background">#2F6FE0</color>

    <!-- ==== Home-screen widgets ====
         RemoteViews cannot host a Compose theme, so the widget's palette is spelled out in
         resources. Every value below is lifted from GridlinkDayColors so the widget and the app
         are the same product; see drawable/widget_card.xml for why these follow the SYSTEM dark
         setting while the app's own palette follows the clock. -->
    <!-- GridlinkDayColors.gradient, start and end. -->
    <color name="widget_card_start">#4DD5F0</color>
    <color name="widget_card_end">#2F6FE0</color>
    <!-- Text drawn straight on the card: header, counter, icon tints. -->
    <color name="widget_on_card">#FFFFFFFF</color>
    <color name="widget_on_card_dim">#B3FFFFFF</color>
    <!-- GridlinkDayColors.listSurface: 55% white, the minimum that carries near-black body text
         over this gradient. -->
    <color name="widget_row_fill">#8CFFFFFF</color>
    <!-- GridlinkDayColors.textPrimary / textSecondary. -->
    <color name="widget_row_text">#0A0F1A</color>
    <color name="widget_row_text_secondary">#3A4A5F</color>
    <!-- GridlinkDayColors.accent, for the unread dot and the counter badge. -->
    <color name="widget_accent">#1B7FE8</color>
    <color name="widget_pill_fill">#33FFFFFF</color>
</resources>
